      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subject: Critical Review of \"Quantum Coherence in room-temperature topological insulators\"\n\nDear Editors,\n\nI have thoroughly reviewed the paper submitted to Nature Physics, titled \"Quantum Coherence in room-temperature topological insulators\". While I found some aspects of the study intriguing, several significant concerns call for a critical examination of their work.\n\nFirstly, the methodology employed raises questions about its reliability. The paper claims to have observed long-lived quantum coherence in a bismuth-based topological insulator at room temperature using a new spectroscopic technique. However, the reported low sample purity could potentially introduce sizable artifacts into the experimental results. Any uncertainty introduced by impurities can direct the observed phenomena, weakening the conviction that these observations truly reflect the inherent properties of the insulating material.\n\nSecondly, the statistical significance of their data appears uncertain, given the relatively large error bars on the measured coherence times. It is unclear whether the observed quantum coherence is robust enough to stand against these uncertainties or if the larger error could dilute the actual coherence period's length. More comprehensive statistical evaluation and wider variability inclusion in the sample set could strengthen the statistical reliability of their findings.\n\nMoreover, the conclusions drawn by the authors appear to depend heavily on the context provided by the precise spectroscopic technique utilized. If",
